# Fleet SDD

This repo uses spec-driven development. Work is defined in artifacts under
`docs/specs/<nnn-slug>/` and gated by a sign-off ledger (`gates.yml`).

## Before writing code

Run `npx fleet-sdd next`. It prints the active stage, the artifact it produces,
the blocking gates and the role that owns each one. Treat it as the authority —
do not infer the state of work by reading the repo.

**No production code without an approved `spec.md`**, and beyond tier `tiny`, an
approved `design.md`. If you cannot name the feature directory your change
belongs to, stop and ask.

## Artifacts

| File | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `spec.md` | Problem, users, scope, acceptance criteria, UX states |
| `design.md` | Approach, data, interfaces, security, performance, observability |
| `tasks.md` | Task table, traceability to criteria, test approach |
| `evidence.md` | What was built, and what was proven |
| `runbook.md` | Alerts, deploy and rollback, support triage |
| `gates.yml` | Who signed off on what |

## Rules

1. Read `.sdd/constitution.md` before touching an artifact. It outranks
   convenience.
2. Read `.sdd/roles/<role>.md` before writing as that role. Check
   `.sdd/overrides/` first — overrides shadow shipped files.
3. Write only into sections the role `owns`. Several roles share each artifact,
   and that only works if each stays in its own sections.
4. Never approve a gate. `fleet-sdd gate <id> approve` is a human accepting
   accountability. Do the work, report it, offer the command.
5. Leave `TODO(sdd)` where you genuinely do not know. Invented content that
   reads as settled is worse than an honest gap.
6. Run `npx fleet-sdd check` before claiming to be finished.

## Commands

```bash
npx fleet-sdd next          # what is next, and who owns it
npx fleet-sdd status        # every feature and its blockers
npx fleet-sdd check         # validate artifacts and gates
npx fleet-sdd roles [id]    # list roles, or print one in full
```

Adding a role is dropping a Markdown file in `.sdd/roles/`. See
`.sdd/EXTENDING.md`.
